

AETHERIS: A Multi‑Scale Integrated Simulation Framework for Unified Astrophysical, Mechanobiological, and Molecular Dynamics Modeling

Author: C. Matthew Blackwell — Independent DesignerLocation: Lafayette, LouisianaDate: July 18, 2026

Abstract

The Aetheris Integrated Simulation Framework (A.I.S.F.) is a multi‑scale computational architecture designed to unify astrophysical fluid dynamics, cellular mechanobiology, and molecular dynamics within a single synchronized simulation fabric. Aetheris introduces a Unified Memory Fabric (U.M.F.) that enables cross‑scale data exchange, temporal synchronization, and emergent behavior detection across macro‑, meso‑, and micro‑scale domains. The system incorporates quantum‑secure encryption for state preservation and supports distributed high‑performance computing deployment. This paper presents the mathematical foundations, architectural design, validation pathway, and experimental results from preliminary module‑level tests. Aetheris represents a new class of multi‑scale simulation frameworks capable of integrating physics, biology, and chemistry in a unified computational environment.

1. Introduction

Multi‑scale phenomena are fundamental to natural systems, yet scientific simulation remains fragmented across domains. Astrophysical simulations operate at kilometer‑to‑parsec scales, mechanobiology at micron‑to‑millimeter scales, and molecular dynamics at angstrom‑to‑nanometer scales. These domains differ in governing equations, numerical methods, and temporal resolution, making cross‑scale integration computationally challenging.

Aetheris proposes a unified architecture capable of synchronizing these domains through a shared computational fabric. The framework integrates:

Navier–Stokes fluid dynamics and N‑body gravity

Finite‑element mechanobiology

Molecular dynamics force‑field engines

A unified memory and synchronization layer

Quantum‑secure encryption and distributed deployment

This paper describes the mathematical foundations, architecture, and validation strategy for Aetheris.

2. Methods

2.1 Macro‑Scale Physics

The astrophysics module solves compressible Navier–Stokes equations coupled with N‑body gravitational acceleration. Spatial discretization uses finite volume methods with adaptive mesh refinement. Time‑stepping obeys the CFL stability condition.

2.2 Meso‑Scale Mechanics

The mechanobiology module uses finite‑element methods to compute stress and strain fields in cellular structures. Constitutive models include linear elasticity and viscoelastic extensions. Intercellular potentials model mechanical coupling between cells.

2.3 Micro‑Scale Dynamics

The molecular dynamics module uses Lennard‑Jones potentials, electrostatic interactions, and Velocity Verlet integration. Rigid‑body constraints support protein and polymer modeling.

2.4 Unified Memory Fabric

The U.M.F. synchronizes modules through:

resolution mapping operators

time‑step harmonization

global state management

encrypted snapshot generation

Downscaling and upscaling operators map macro → meso → micro and micro → meso → macro fields.

2.5 Encryption Layer

AES‑256‑GCM and CRYSTALS‑Kyber secure simulation states. A homomorphic compute zone enables encrypted analysis.

2.6 Deployment Fabric

Aetheris supports multi‑node HPC clusters with GPU acceleration, FEM‑optimized nodes, and MD‑optimized nodes. MPI and gRPC manage inter‑node communication.

3. Results

3.1 Module‑Level Validation

Preliminary tests show:

Astrophysics module reproduces standard N‑body benchmarks.

Mechanobiology module matches FEM deformation curves from experimental data.

Molecular dynamics module produces stable trajectories and energy profiles consistent with established MD engines.

3.2 Cross‑Scale Behavior

Initial coupling tests demonstrate:

macro‑scale pressure fields influence meso‑scale stress patterns

meso‑scale mechanical tension alters micro‑scale conformational states

micro‑scale energy changes propagate upward to meso‑scale material properties

These results confirm the feasibility of cross‑scale synchronization.

3.3 Stability and Conservation

Across all modules:

mass, momentum, and energy conservation hold within numerical tolerance

time‑stepping remains stable under realistic parameter ranges

emergent patterns appear in coupled simulations

4. Discussion

Aetheris demonstrates that multi‑scale integration across physics, biology, and chemistry is computationally feasible when supported by a unified memory fabric and synchronized time‑stepping. The architecture enables emergent behavior detection across domains that traditionally operate in isolation.

The introduction of quantum‑secure encryption ensures reproducibility and tamper‑proof provenance, addressing growing concerns in scientific data integrity.

Aetheris represents a new class of simulation frameworks capable of supporting interdisciplinary research in astrophysics, mechanobiology, molecular science, and computational physics.

5. Limitations

Full multi‑scale simulations require significant HPC resources.

Cross‑scale operators must be tuned for specific scientific domains.

Biological and molecular models may require domain‑specific calibration.

Long‑term stability under extreme parameter regimes remains under investigation.

6. Conclusion

Aetheris provides a unified computational architecture for multi‑scale simulation across astrophysical, biological, and molecular domains. Preliminary results demonstrate module‑level accuracy, cross‑scale consistency, and stable emergent behavior. Future work includes full‑scale HPC deployment, expanded biological modeling, and peer‑reviewed validation.

